"""Template for the ISO 226:2023 Table 1 coefficients.

**Nothing in this project runs without these.** They are the per-frequency
coefficients of ISO 226:2023 Formula (1), and the formula cannot be evaluated
without them -- unlike the Annex B fixture, which only disables one test.

They are ISO's, not this project's, and are not redistributable. To use the
generator, copy this file to `reference/iso226_table1.py` and fill in the three
columns of Table 1 from your own copy of the standard:

    https://www.iso.org/standard/83117.html

Each list holds 29 values, in order, at the ISO 266 preferred third-octave
frequencies from 20 Hz to 12.5 kHz:

    20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80, 100, 125, 160,
    200, 250, 315, 400, 500, 630, 800, 1000, 1250, 1600,
    2000, 2500, 3150, 4000, 5000, 6300, 8000, 10000, 12500

Use the **third edition** values. ISO 226:2003 is not interchangeable: every
alpha_f changed when the loudness exponent at 1 kHz was revised from 0.25 to
0.30, several L_U values moved by 0.1 dB, and Formula (1) itself changed.

The loader checks that each column has 29 entries and that L_U at 1 kHz is
exactly 0.0 -- which it is by definition, since L_U is specified relative to
1 kHz. That catches an off-by-one during transcription, the likeliest mistake.

If you do not hold a copy of the standard, the presets in `PEQ/` and `REW/`
were generated
with these coefficients and are usable as they are; only regenerating them or
building new levels needs this file.
"""

# alpha_f -- exponent of loudness perception (Table 1, column 2)
ISO_AF = [
]

# L_U -- magnitude of the linear transfer function normalized at 1 kHz, dB
# (Table 1, column 3). The 1 kHz entry, index 17, is 0.0.
ISO_LU = [
]

# T_f -- threshold of hearing, dB (Table 1, column 4)
ISO_TF = [
]
